[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "ADR-2608190100"
  :adr/title "A held resident run is not on the model, cannot be answered, and must not stop the other Bots"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-19"
  :adr/body
  "## What happened

Measured on the resident deployment, 2026-08-19T05:01Z. The startup workforce
was installed and healthy by every surface that reports on it: 8 businesses,
70 Bots, 70 enabled, provider `murakumo` reviewed and authenticated, 72 of 74
Bots reading `idle`. It had submitted nothing for 18 hours 34 minutes, and 66
of its 70 jobs were past due.

Nothing reported a fault, because from each surface's own point of view there
was none. `bots_list` showed idle Bots, which was true. The tick thread was
alive and had used 34ms of CPU in 145 seconds, which was correct behaviour for
a pass that starts nothing. `live-sessions` returned a live `:passkey` session
whose user owned all 70 jobs. `workforce-job-due?` said 66 were due.

The count that mattered was `active 1` against `max-active 1`, and the one run
holding it was `workforce-run-e2ec934c` on `nexus x402 / Sales`, status
`:held` since 2026-08-18T10:32:08Z — five minutes after the last tick anyone
saw.

## Why that run was held

The Goal provider must record a plan of one to eight steps, and ADR-2608151900
admits `goal_complete` only once every step carries a host action receipt. The
resident objective also says, correctly, that a tick which finds nothing safe
to do should record the evidence for a no-op and complete.

The provider planned two steps: inspect the repository root, then record the
no-op and complete. Step one executed `workspace_list`, produced a receipt and
passed its verifier. Step two was a conclusion — it ran no tool, because
concluding is not a tool — so it could never carry a receipt, so the goal could
never complete. The run reported, accurately:

  The host verifier requires a host execution receipt for step s2, but no tool
  was executed for that step (it is a no-op conclusion step).

Both rules are right. Their conjunction has no exit.

## Three defects, and only the third one is why it spread

1. **`blocked` and `waiting-approval` were recorded as one status.** They are
   not one outcome. `waiting-approval` leaves a card a person can decide;
   `blocked` means the provider stopped and asked, and for an unattended tick
   there is nobody being asked.

2. **A `:held` goal job could not be reached by anything.** `cancel!` requires
   an in-memory turn, which no longer exists after a restart, so the MCP and
   HTTP cancel paths answered `run-not-found`. `recover-interrupted!` requeues
   `#{:queued :leased :running :checkpointed}` and does not include `:held`. A
   hold therefore survived every restart with nothing able to end it.

3. **The capacity count treated a hold as if it were on the model.**
   `max-active` exists to stop resident ticks overlapping on a capacity-one
   provider — `murakumo-main` reports `parallel: 1` — so what it must count is
   runs consuming that provider. A held run consumes none. This is the defect
   that turned one stuck Bot into a stopped company.

## Decision

`goal-run-status` maps a visible state onto a run status and takes the
resident flag as its second fact. A resident `blocked` becomes `:failed`,
which is honest — the tick did not reach its goal — and `:failed -> :queued`
is the one legal way back out of a terminal state, so the job simply runs
again at its cadence. `waiting-approval` stays `:held` for both, and an
interactive `blocked` stays `:held` because somebody is watching it.

`workforce-bot-inferring?` is separated from `workforce-bot-active?`.
`max-active` counts the first; the per-Bot refusal keeps using the second, so
a Bot whose run is held still gets no second run while every other Bot
proceeds.

`recover-interrupted!` closes a resident `:held` run that has no outstanding
approval card, as `:cancelled` with `:agent.run/error-type :hold-unanswerable`.
`:cancelled` rather than `:rejected` because no person refused this work.
A hold with a card outstanding is left alone: it is answerable, and closing it
would discard a decision somebody was asked for.

## Not done here

The plan contract still permits a step that no tool can satisfy. Forbidding a
conclusion step — completion is not a step — belongs with the Goal plan
contract in ADR-2608151900 and is a separate change. Until it lands, such a
tick now fails and retries instead of wedging the fleet, which is a smaller
fault and a visible one.

`max-active` was NOT raised. The provider serves one slot; raising the app's
cap would queue at the provider instead, and the stall was never throughput.

## Verification

Three tests, each shown to fail on the unfixed code:

  a-blocked-resident-tick-is-failed-and-a-held-one-is-still-held
  one-held-bot-does-not-stop-the-others
  startup-closes-a-resident-hold-that-nobody-can-answer

`resident-workforce-does-not-overlap-an-active-job-across-bots` keeps
asserting that a job actually on the model stops every other Bot; it now
redefines the var that decides capacity. That invariant is unchanged."}]
